		{
			dependencies: ["663739E71D834D27"]
			description: [
				"With a tree chopping setup pumping out &6logs&r you can look towards automating your &nlivingwood needs&r."
				""
				"Deployers are still a bit out of reach &7(unless you rushed them)&r but &adispensers&r, thanks to &dQuark&r, can place blocks."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"With your &atree farm&r, the logs can be belted into some dispensers, and &bpulsed with redstone&r to place around a pure daisy."
				""
				"You now have a way to automatically &6start a pure daisy conversion cycle&r!"
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Now to collect the living wood you'll need to know &o&nwhen&r you can break the blocks. "
				""
				"&a&nTwo ways you can do this:&r "
				""
				"&6A timer&r, &7via create's pulse repeater&r, set to &n~1 minute&r."
				""
				"&6Direct detection&r with a &dsmart observer&r filtered to the target conversion block, or a vanilla observer with &cbinary counters&r set to throw an output after a set number of updates."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"Once you have the &atimer or the direct detection&r, a redstone signal from either can then be wired into your &6chosen collection method&r. "
				""
				"This could be some moving create contraption with &adrills&r, or &adrills&r dedicated to each pure daisy tile, or something else, probably with &adrills&r."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"&n&6&lBut notice what we did here&r&r. This &ccomplex task&r was &abroken up into smaller problems&r to solve, all of which you had choices on how to handle. "
				""
				"&n&b&lThis is a very important principle&r&r when it comes to taking on more complicated automation tasks later in the pack."
				""
				"Instead of viewing the &centire monolith of the task&r, &n&areally try to break down the steps&r&r, see what the task actually asks of you. You will often find that it is &6easier&r than anticipated."
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"&bDeliver&r → &6Place&r → &aClock &7(or) &aDetect&r → &dBreak&r → &cCollect&r → &4Loop Forever&r →"
			]
